"""Colour phosh's battery icon by how fast the battery is actually moving.

The phone shows a percentage and a lightning bolt, and both look the same
whether 1 watt or 6 are going in. That is the one number worth seeing at a
glance - a bad cable, a laptop port or a charger that has quietly renegotiated
all look like "charging" and cost hours.

So: green while it is going in fast, amber in the middle, red when it is
barely moving. Optionally the same on battery, where the meaning is reversed -
green is a phone that is drawing little.

How the colour gets there: through the widget in phosh's own top bar
(phosh-battery-time/ next door), which also shows the time left. This
program decides the colour and writes it into one small file in the runtime
directory; the widget puts it on the battery icon's image, with a stylesheet
on that one image and on nothing else.

It used to go through the THEME, and that is worth remembering as the way
not to do it. Switching org.gnome.desktop.interface gtk-theme to generated
themes of our own restyled phosh at runtime - and broke what was not ours:
phosh applies the accent colour only to the themes it knows by name, so the
quick settings went blue, and Flatpak apps that did not know the name lost
their dark mode (found and proved on 24.9.2026). No desktop setting is
written any more, except the list of status-icon plugins that the widget
has to be on to be loaded at all.

Nothing here needs root. It reads files in /sys and writes two files in the
runtime directory.
"""

